WEBVTT

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:06.401
In our previous video
we added a ray from

00:00:06.413 --> 00:00:10.600
the main camera through
to our mouse position

00:00:10.601 --> 00:00:14.160
so we could actually find
where the clicks were happening.

00:00:14.161 --> 00:00:17.129
So to do this to detect
objects we need a physics.

00:00:17.141 --> 00:00:19.300
raycast so you can
send that out into

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:24.200
the physics world and return true if you
hit a collider and that's any game object

00:00:24.201 --> 00:00:26.520
which would have a
collider within the game.

00:00:26.521 --> 00:00:32.520
So what we can do below our debug dot draw
ray is we can create a new if statement

00:00:32.521 --> 00:00:35.600
because we're going to need to
be able to check if this is happening.

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:38.280
So we'll often up brackets and we'll say
physics because we're going to use the

00:00:38.281 --> 00:00:41.828
physics system dot
raycast and raycast it

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.920
casts a ray from an
origin point in a direction

00:00:45.921 --> 00:00:48.372
similar when we wanted
to draw the ray along

00:00:48.384 --> 00:00:51.680
a distance and it checks
colliders within the scene.

00:00:51.681 --> 00:00:56.400
So it's raycast with a capital R but we can
open up the brackets and because we already

00:00:56.524 --> 00:01:02.120
found the position of the ray because we
had that local variable we'll write ray and

00:01:02.121 --> 00:01:09.480
then we'll write comma out raycast hit
and then we'll just give this a short hand

00:01:09.654 --> 00:01:13.425
of hit and what out means
it just returns a value to the

00:01:13.426 --> 00:01:17.120
variable that we want so
that we can begin to use it.

00:01:17.121 --> 00:01:20.640
So instead the if statement for the physics
dot raycast then we want to have another

00:01:22.109 --> 00:01:29.960
if statement and say if hit dot transform
is equal to the transform of the object that

00:01:29.961 --> 00:01:35.120
this is then in curly braces
will say destroy game object.

00:01:35.121 --> 00:01:37.824
So again if the
raycast hits a transform

00:01:37.836 --> 00:01:40.960
we're checking if it is
this one where we have

00:01:40.961 --> 00:01:43.381
this script currently
on and then if it is

00:01:43.393 --> 00:01:46.000
we'll destroy that game
object and with a hit

00:01:46.001 --> 00:01:48.651
dot transform you
can have things like hit

00:01:48.663 --> 00:01:51.580
dot collider hit dot
point or hit dot distance

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:57.200
problem is without this check here the hit
dot transform is equal to transform if with

00:01:57.201 --> 00:02:02.220
any script with target we'll run and it will
destroy whenever the raycast is hit even

00:02:02.221 --> 00:02:04.243
if it's a different target
so that can make it more

00:02:04.244 --> 00:02:07.760
difficult and you get
outcomes that you don't expect.

00:02:07.761 --> 00:02:12.440
So make sure you save this script now we're
going to hit play now you can see wherever

00:02:12.441 --> 00:02:15.295
there's an object and
we actually managed to

00:02:15.307 --> 00:02:18.240
click where removing
that object that appears

00:02:18.241 --> 00:02:21.840
so we're just destroying that
when we see it in the game.

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:24.476
Now as an example if we
remove that if statement

00:02:24.488 --> 00:02:26.640
which check to see
if the hit transform

00:02:26.641 --> 00:02:32.200
was the same and we just put destroy in the
original if statement we've got our game

00:02:32.201 --> 00:02:36.480
playing and if I click it will remove all
of the different objects within the scene

00:02:36.481 --> 00:02:39.239
because it hits any of
them with this particular

00:02:39.251 --> 00:02:43.760
script on it and that's not good for
the core concept of our gameplay.

00:02:43.761 --> 00:02:47.680
So you always want to make sure that you're
checking to see if the hit transform is the

00:02:47.681 --> 00:02:51.520
same as the one that we're using
in any particular object at that time.

00:02:51.521 --> 00:02:54.392
Just as a recap I want
you to be in your target

00:02:54.404 --> 00:02:59.289
script and then within your update method
within the lines that we created for the

00:02:59.301 --> 00:03:04.335
debug and drawing the ray we need to make
sure that we have a new line which is an if

00:03:04.347 --> 00:03:07.200
statement of if physics
dot raycast in brackets

00:03:07.201 --> 00:03:10.649
a ray comma out
raycast hit and then the

00:03:10.661 --> 00:03:14.640
variable name of hit
and then within the curly

00:03:14.641 --> 00:03:19.360
braces we'll have another if statement that
hit dot transform is equal to the transform

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.101
of this particular
script or object that this

00:03:22.113 --> 00:03:24.440
script is on will
destroy that particular

00:03:24.441 --> 00:03:24.972
object because we know
we're only clicking on

00:03:24.973 --> 00:03:35.280
that one that's in the
mouse position is on.